  "intro": {
    "language": "en",
    "text": "Georgina Hope Rinehart (née Hancock, born 9 February 1954) is an Australian  mining magnate, businesswoman and heiress. She is the chairwoman of Hancock Prospecting, a privately owned mining company founded by her father, Lang Hancock.\nRinehart was born in Perth, and spent her early years in the Pilbara region. She boarded at St Hilda's Anglican School for Girls. After dropping out of University of Sydney, she went to work at Hancock Prospecting. When Lang Hancock died in 1992, Rinehart succeeded him as the new chair, as Lang Hancock's only child.\nIn the 2010s, Rinehart began investing in areas outside the mining industry, such as in Ten Network Holdings and Fairfax Media, and bought several cattle stations; she sold her shares in Fairfax and all cattle stations within a decade.\nRinehart is Australia's richest person. Her wealth reached around A$29 billion in 2012, at which point she overtook Christy Walton as the world's richest woman and was included on the Forbes list of The World's 100 Most Powerful Women. Rinehart's net worth dropped significantly over the following few years due to a slowdown in the Australian mining sector. Forbes estimated her net worth in 2019 at US$14.8 billion...",
    "sourceId": "wikipedia-en",
    "usage": "Research excerpt; paraphrase and cross-check before publication."
  },
